  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "316",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        39.52751922607422,
        57.665863037109375,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        78.57550048828125,
        39.52751922607422,
        316.3913879394531,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and hence it may be adding unnecessary complication to build the tempered distributions in all dimensions.",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        57.378700256347656,
        438.23138427734375,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this case, PMCMC sampling gives the option to explore some parameters by using MCMC sampling",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        67.3412857055664,
        438.22943115234375,
        75.65313720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "while exploring others (such as those which are highly correlated or non-identiﬁable) with SMC sampling,",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        77.30387115478516,
        438.2330017089844,
        85.61572265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and hence limit variance in the SMC importance weights. There are several options for performing this in",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        87.2664566040039,
        438.2213134765625,
        95.57830810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the PMCMC framework: both the particle Gibbs and the particle Metropolis–Hastings variants could be",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        97.22904205322266,
        438.2285461425781,
        105.5408935546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "used; the choice largely depends on the correlation between the identiﬁable and non-identiﬁable subsets of",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        107.1916275024414,
        438.2303771972656,
        115.50347900390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "variables. In conclusion, we feel that, as much as PMCMC sampling provides Monte Carlo solutions to",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        117.15421295166016,
        438.2222900390625,
        125.466064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a unique class of problems, it also provides a ﬂexible framework allowing practitioners to mix and match",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        127.1167984008789,
        438.2231750488281,
        135.42864990234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Monte Carlo strategies to suit their particular application.",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        137.0793914794922,
        263.3550109863281,
        145.3912353515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Olivier Cappé (Telecom ParisTech and Centre National de la Recherche Scientiﬁque, Paris)",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        157.00450134277344,
        380.2177429199219,
        165.31634521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Icongratulatetheauthorsforthisimpressivepieceofworkwhich,Ibelieve,isaverysigniﬁcantcontribution",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        166.9670867919922,
        438.22857666015625,
        175.2789306640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to the toolbox of Markov chain Monte Carlo and sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) methods.",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        176.92967224121094,
        388.6605529785156,
        185.24151611328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For brevity, I focus on the particle independent Metropolis–Hastings (PIMH) algorithm which is the",
      "bbox": [
        51.676300048828125,
        186.89222717285156,
        438.23028564453125,
        195.20407104492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "basic building block for the other samplers that are presented in the paper. Although theorem 2 also",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        196.8547821044922,
        438.22857666015625,
        205.1666259765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "covers the more involved case of SMC sampling, the core idea is the auxiliary construction which shows",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        206.8173370361328,
        438.23040771484375,
        215.12918090820312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that a proper Markov chain Monte Carlo algorithm may be obtained from sampling–importance re-",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        216.77989196777344,
        438.22857666015625,
        225.09173583984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sampling (Rubin, 1987), irrespectively of the number N of particles. This idea, however, does seem to be",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        226.74244689941406,
        438.2232971191406,
        235.05429077148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "quite different both from the multiple-try (Liu et al., 2000) and the pseudomarginal (Beaumont, 2003)",
      "bbox": [
        42.70989990234375,
        236.7050018310547,
        438.2268371582031,
        245.016845703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "approaches and I encourage the authors to discuss in more detail its connections, if any, with earlier ideas",
      "bbox": [
        42.71080017089844,
        246.6675567626953,
        438.2340393066406,
        254.97940063476562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in the literature.",
      "bbox": [
        42.71080017089844,
        256.6300964355469,
        103.26090240478516,
        264.94195556640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fig. 3 (in Section 3.1) is very promising as it suggests that the approach is practicable in large dimensional",
      "bbox": [
        51.67720031738281,
        266.5926513671875,
        438.2295227050781,
        274.9045104980469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "settings for which a ‘causal’ factorization of the likelihood is available. In particular, I wonder whether it is",
      "bbox": [
        42.71080017089844,
        276.5552062988281,
        438.22418212890625,
        284.8670654296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "possible to predict the relationship between the dimension T and the number N of particles that is implicit",
      "bbox": [
        42.71080017089844,
        286.51776123046875,
        438.225830078125,
        294.8296203613281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in Fig. 3. In an attempt to answer this question, I conducted a toy numerical experiment in the spirit of the",
      "bbox": [
        42.7108154296875,
        296.4803161621094,
        438.2275390625,
        304.79217529296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "scaling construction (Roberts and Rosenthal, 2001), where the target πT is a product probability density",
      "bbox": [
        42.7108154296875,
        305.689697265625,
        438.2237548828125,
        316.10137939453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "function and SMC sampling is also carried out by using successive independent proposals—clearly, the",
      "bbox": [
        42.70947265625,
        316.4071960449219,
        438.23175048828125,
        324.71905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
